-      "content": "*So pretexto de* (under the pretext of) introduces an official justification the writer is signaling as doubtful -- distinct from a neutral connector, it carries built-in skepticism about whether the stated reason was the real one. Exactly the tool for describing Cold War interventions whose declared goals rarely matched their actual motives. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know how to report statements with *según* + a source and with *decir que* from B1 Unit 13, and how the tense moves back after a past verb (*dijo que quería*). This is useful for foreign interventions, where governments, documents and historians tell different stories."
